<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marinus of Tyre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 70–130 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marinus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> was a Greek geographer and mapmaker of the Roman period, active around 100 CE. His work is lost but known through Ptolemy, who used and criticised it in his Geography. Marinus is credited with introducing a systematic grid of latitude and longitude for maps, and he recorded travels such as those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-maternus">Julius Maternus</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marinus-of-tyre.jpg" alt="Marinus of Tyre" width="320" height="509" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gerard Mercator (after Ptolemy); Gottfried Kempen (publisher) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marinus_e_Ptolomeu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
